  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nAs a cold front pushes through central and northeastern SD this \nmorning, it will be interacting will a small bit of moisture and \nlittle bit of instability. This will cause some scattered showers, \nand maybe a thunderstorm or two, to form mainly over northern SD \nearly this morning. There will be some drier air between the surface \nand the showers aloft, which could cause the rain to evaporated \nbefore reaching the surface. So, while there might be some showers \nthat move through, there might not much rain reaching the surface \nthis morning. The colder air moving in behind the cold front will \nhelp to cool temperatures to be around normal for this time of the \nyear, with highs in the low 70s to low 80s today. A bit of higher \nsurface pressure will be moving in today into Thursday morning. \nSouthwesterly flow behind the high pressure will stick around over \ncentral and northeastern SD Thursday, helping to move some warm, dry \nair in. With the high pressure today and the dry air Thursday, \nshower and storm chances will stay out of central and northeastern \nSD. \n\nThe drier air moving in Thursday will cause some elevated fire \nweather concerns over central and portions of northeastern SD, as \nthe minimum relative humidity over areas west of the Missouri River \nwill be between 15-25%. The one thing keeping things from becoming \ntoo concerning is that there will be slightly weaker winds over the \nareas of lowest humidity Thursday afternoon. There is only a 20-40% \nchance of winds gusting 25 mph or more in portions of Corson and \nLyman counties, with the rest of the counties west of the Missouri \nRiver having less than a 10% chance that the winds will be able to \ngust 25 mph or more. There is high Grassland Fire Danger Index Value \ncovering Corson and Dewey counties because of how dry it will be and \nhow easy it could be for a fire to ignite and spread with all the \ndry fuels. The winds and the humidity will need to have an eye kept \non it over the next couple of model runs to see if the fire weather \nconcerns increase. If it does, a Red Flag warning might be needed. \nThe James River Valley will see the stronger winds Thursday \nafternoon, with 30-60% chance of wind gusting up to 30+ mph. Though \nthe minimum relative humidity will be between 30-45%, reducing the \nfire weather concerns to the east of the Missouri River.\n\nAs a small upper-level ridge/shortwave moves over SD Thursday night \ninto Friday, another surface front will be moving through central \nand northeastern SD. A little bit of moisture will be sneaking up \nover northeastern SD late Thursday night into Friday, which will be \nable to interact with the front to cause some showers and storms to \ndevelop Friday afternoon into the evening over portions of \nnortheastern SD. Models do vary how quickly they move the front \nthrough central and northeastern SD, which then varies how much of \nthe area could see showers and storms. The models that move the \nfront through quickly have it out of northeastern SD by the \nafternoon, keeping precipitation chances to the south and east of \nnortheastern SD. Other models have the front move through slower, \nwhich would then give more area in northeastern SD to build up \ninstability and moisture for storm development during the afternoon \nthrough the overnight periods. Behind this cold front and into the \nweekend, temperatures look to drop slightly below normal. The next \nchance for precipitation will come late Saturday night into Monday \nwith more moisture moves into the area before a surface low and \nfrontal boundaries moves in. While models currently have widespread \n50-80% chance for 0.5 inch falling Sunday and Monday, models still \nvary the timing and location of the surface low and associated \nfronts as well as the precipitation that comes along with it. This \ncauses more widespread chances further out in the forecast that \ntypically shrink in area and magnitude as it gets closer. This \nprecipitation chances will need to be watched as we get closer to \nthe weekend.",
